ver two dollars  with a single pound but today you can  only get and fifteen cents with a single  pound when you consider these shifts and  currency values apple's price hikes  while still insane make a little bit  more sense but this brings up the  question why in the world are european  currencies doing so bad  taking a look back this is clearly not  the new trend this downtrend has been  forming for well over a decade and the  recent issues have simply worsened the  situation so what set off this chain of  events  well one of the biggest fundamental  issues plaguing the euro since its  creation is the fact that it's not just  representative of one country initially  this was seen as a benefit the euro  promised to unify european countries  economically and to make trade between  these neighboring countries easier than  ever before this unification also  promised to make europe as a whole a  stronger economic force against the us  but the problem with the unified  currency is that it's virtually  impossible to set economic policies that  are appropriate for every member you see  with america while the various states  have differences culturally and  politically they're extremely  homogeneous economically when  californian businesses are suffering  from a bear market so are taxing  businesses similarly when new york  businesses are booming it's more likely  that floridian businesses are also  booming so it makes sense for all these  states to use the same currency and for  the fed to set overarching economic  policies the same however cannot be said  about the members of the eurozone and  especially the poorly performing members  over the past 15 years this has  primarily been spain and degrees greece  basically got wiped out by the 2009  financial crisis if the eu didn't bail  them out greece would have defaulted on  their debt and spiraled into a  depression during the worst of it  reese's budget deficit was equivalent to  12.9 percent of their gdp for  perspective that would be equivalent to  the us having a budget deficit of 2.7  trillion dollars per year at first  greece tried to play it cool and assured  everyone that they would get their debt  in check within two years but just four  months later greece would admit that  they could default at any moment around  the same time spain was going through  their own economic crisis  like greece spain's economy was  devastated by the 2008 financial  recession and they were spiraling into a  depression as an outside observer  americans could feel bad for these  countries and their people but  economically speaking it they weren't  all that affected the same however could  not be said about eurozone countries who  had skin in the game greece and spain  were already massive dead weights  bringing down the euro but if these  countries collapsed oh boy it would be  bad so the prosperous eurozone countries  had no choice but to bail out this  failing economies the eu and the imf  would provide 240 billion euros worth of  emergency funds to greece and 100  billion euro rescue package to spain  and where do you think all this money  came from it of course came from  quantitative easing also known as money  printing since 2008 the euro money  supply has literally doubled to make  things worse the bailouts weren't all  that impactful either sure they helped  these countries stay afloat and did not  default but fundamentally they didn't  change anything and the underlying  economies would just continue to get  worse  while spain and greece were undergoing  economic hell many eu countries like the  uk and germany were actually recovering  extraordinarily well their unemployment  graphs were plummeting and their need  for money printing was dwindling however  they couldn't stop the money printing  because certain eurozone countries like  spain and greece were still very much  feeling the pain  in both spain and degrees unemployment  would rocket to 25 to 30 percent for  perspective unemployment didn't cost 25  even during the worst of the great  depression  so these countries were no longer just  experiencing a financial crisis  they were in a full-fledged economic  depression and this was painfully  evident in their gdp numbers spain's gdp  would fall from a peak of 1.6 trillion  dollars to just under 1.2 trillion  dollars similarly greece's gdp would  fall from a peak of 354 billion dollars  to 192 billion dollars  even today neither of these countries  have been able to return to their peak  gdps and this becomes even worse when  you consider that europe has been  experiencing record inflation meaning  that their inflation-adjusted gdps are  even worse  also while i've mainly been picking on  greece in spain throughout this video  these guys were by no means the only  perpetrators though they were the worst  some of the other countries dragging  down the euro included cyprus portugal  italy slovakia and even france  this left the ecb in an extremely  difficult situation what were they  supposed to do  on one side they have countries like the  uk germany and denmark where  unemployment was reaching historically  low levels these countries indicated  that it was time to chill out on the  money printing and potentially even  start quantitative tightening however on  the other end of the spectrum you had  the perpetrators who were in a more dire  state than ever these countries  indicated that it might even be time to  pump up the money printing the ecb would  end up siding with the countries that  needed help and they would keep economic  policies loose while this was probably a  godsend for the struggling countries the  prosperous countries that weren't  exactly happy and rightfully so the  prosperous countries didn't really  benefit all that much from the loose  economic policies but they were very  much harmed by its accompanying side  effects they were starting to feel that  they bought significantly more to the  table than they were receiving and this  feeling is what would eventually evolve  into brexit in june of 2016 britain  would vote on leaving the eu and after  years of negotiations and discussions  brexit would finally take place on  january 31st 2020. much of england felt  that they had finally gotten a fresh  start and that they were no longer way  down by the eu  but little did they know that pandemic  was just around the corner  starting off with the most obvious  economic side effect of the pandemic we  have the money printing people often  like to criticize the us for their  excessive money printing as they should  but what people often forget is that the  whole world has been printing just as  much money if not even more take the uk  for example who has printed 895 billion  pounds at the average conversion rate  over the past two years this is  equivalent to 1.14 trillion dollars  now the u.s has grown its money supply  by a far larger amount coming in at  roughly 6.2 trillion dollars but you  have to look at it in relative terms  compared to the american gdp of 21  trillion dollars american printing works  out to roughly 30 percent of their gdp  compared to the english gdp of 2.7  trillion dollars however english  printing works out to 42 of their gdp  that's clearly a problem the ecb on the  other hand hasn't been nearly as  aggressive with their money printing but  they've been much more lenient when it  comes to interest rates the ecp  literally maintained a negative interest  rate into 2022 and they're just now  starting to aggressively hike rates  all of that extra relative printing from  the uk and the loose economic policies  from the ecb have to be reflected  somewhere and that somewhere seems to be  conversion rates economic policies  aren't the only battle that europe has  been fighting though they've also been  hit hard by russian sanctions  you see it europe has historically been  extremely dependent on russia for oil  and natural gas given the russian  sanctions europe has been forced to find  new energy partners which has been  anything but easy the problem is that  the entire world knows that europe is  desperate for energy which means that  sellers can charge a premium and that's  exactly what they've been doing gas  prices across the notable european  countries are well above 5 per gallon if  not over six dollars per gallon a  similar story can be seen with  electricity prices as well  main wallet russia is simply adding fuel  to the fire by essentially retaliating  against the sanctions you see europe has  yet to fully cut off russia they still  receive a significant amount of crude  oil through pipelines like the nord  stream 1. this specific pipeline was  closed in july for 10 days for regular  maintenance but when the pipeline was  reopened it was only operating at 20  capacity russia would close down the  pipeline again at the end of august  citing an apparent leak and russia has  yet to reopen this pipeline as of  september 8. while russia would like to  convince us that there's a leak it seems  like russia is simply halting overflow  on purpose to spike energy prices in  europe as europeans are forced to spend  more on our energy they're naturally  gonna pull back on discretionary  spending and given that the central  banks are also starting to pull back  this sets up the perfect storm for an  economic disaster  at the end of the day the euro was  created with good intentions it aimed to  unify europe economically and create a  global powerhouse however a major  shortfall of the system is the vast  inequality between the member countries  this has forced the prosperous countries  to constantly pop up the failing  countries which hasn't been particularly  helpful for either of them the uk has  decided to take this issue into their  own hands and become the first country  to leave the eu  but this hasn't worked out all that well  either the pandemic along with the  russian sanctions have taken a massive  toll on all european countries and most  of them are simply fighting to put off a  nasty recession and bring down inflation  compared to this the us is actually  doing really well we've been ahead of  the curve when it comes to economic  tightening and the recession isn't that  bad at least not yet so naturally the us  dollar has been destroying european  currencies and by the looks of it that's  not going to be changing it anytime soon  so europe better get used to those  iphone price hikes because they're  likely permanent but that's just what i  think what do you think about european  iphone price hacks comment that down  below also drop a like if you hope that  europe can turn things around and of  course consider joining our discord  community to suggest future video ideas  and consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm mari and i'll see you guys on  the next one

